{"kind":"assert","text":"G2 per-metric age, decided (answers 850ce256 and 2ebc8b89). Take the bounded same-scope fact-time projection. Not two more clocks, and no raised value or effect allowances: the limits are the design's and a layer's age is already recorded, as the entry each metric's fact ref names.\n\nThe form. A declared projection field may name one of its item's fact-ref slots and yield the sealed time of the entry that ref names, read at the exact head the projection is read at; null when the slot is empty. The ref's `under` fixes the scope, so the read is one named entry in the same scope's journal, never a lookup in another scope, a journal window, or a time the host invents. The validator refuses the field when the slot is not a fact ref of that item or its `under` is not the projection's scope. The field exposes the entry's time only, not its fields or signer: a reader who may read the item may read when its recorded fact was sealed. Replay reproduces it from the journal, so A2's replay and the archive are unaffected.\n\nWhat G2 uses it for. Moisture layer age from `moistureAt`, scouting layer age from `noteAt` (and `insectsAt` where the note is absent, the later of the two if both), temperature from the zone's `summaryTime` as today. `probeTime` stays the bench's overall time for the carousel's ordering and the stale rule; it is never a layer's age. An absent metric keeps its old age.\n\nWitnesses. The scope-owning test (packages/derive or packages/scope, where the projection is derived) shows: the field equals the named entry's time at the read head; a probe of another metric leaves it unchanged; an empty slot reads null; a declaration naming a non-fact slot or a foreign `under` is refused by name. The read-authorization witness: the same session read that serves the item serves the field, and a session that may not read the scope gets the same refusal as for the item. Name both in the review request. If the form cannot carry the field without touching the read wire's bounds (APPLICATION_PROJECTION_WIRE_BOUNDS), say so before changing them.\n\nThe model boundary. The scripted typed-text tool consumer in the kit's test host is the reproducible witness for \"through the model\": derived schema, exact values, one native signature, quiet context. Label it as scripted. A separate run with an actual model may be reported as a labelled observation, not as a gate. Nothing in the host is called a model.\n\nThe grow note's 6.2 gains the parameter form and this field when planner next amends it; G2 does not edit the note.","body":{"to":"builder"}}